1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,962
Chapter 64: Psychiatric Disorders.

3
00:00:05,962 --> 00:00:27,962
Psychological Adjustments To Pregnancy Classification Of Psychiatric Disorders Major Depression Bipolar And Related Disorders Anxiety Disorders Schizophrenia Spectrum Disorders Eating Disorders Substance Use Disorder Posttraumatic Stress Disorders Personality Disorders References Pregnancy and the puerperium are at times sufficiently stressful to provoke mental illness.

4
00:00:27,962 --> 00:00:36,950
Such illness may represent recurrence or exacerbation of a preexisting psychiatric disorder, or it may signal the onset of a new condition.

5
00:00:36,950 --> 00:00:43,000
Especially prevalent in pregnancy and the puerperium are depressive and anxiety disorders .

6
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:52,825
Many of these disorders are associated with less prenatal care, substance use, preterm delivery, and other poor obstetrical and neonatal outcomes.

7
00:00:52,825 --> 00:00:58,312
In affected gravidas, the postpartum psychiatric illness rate also is higher .

8
00:00:58,312 --> 00:01:03,950
Last, perinatal mood disorders can adversely hinder the mother-child relationship.

9
00:01:03,950 --> 00:01:12,612
Despite these known risks, obstetrical providers often are reluctant to confront or fail to identify mental health issues during pregnancy.

10
00:01:12,612 --> 00:01:21,587
Lyell and colleagues (2012) found that the diagnosis of depression was not documented in nearly half of the records of affected women.

11
00:01:21,587 --> 00:01:24,862
Psychological Adjustments To Pregnancy.

12
00:01:24,862 --> 00:01:32,462
Biochemical factors and life stressors can markedly influence mental health and mental illness during the perinatal period .

13
00:01:32,462 --> 00:01:38,275
Intuitively, pregnancy exacerbates some coexisting psychological disorders.

14
00:01:38,275 --> 00:01:54,275
Namely, a higher risk for mood disorders is linked with pregnancy-related shifts in sex steroid and monoamine neurotransmitter levels, dysfunction of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis, thyroid dysfunction, and immune response alterations .

15
00:01:54,275 --> 00:02:06,100
These changes, coupled with familial clustering of depression cases, suggest that a subgroup of women may be at greater risk for developing a unipolar major depressive disorder during pregnancy.

16
00:02:06,100 --> 00:02:09,975
Women respond variably to the stresses of pregnancy.

17
00:02:09,975 --> 00:02:18,012
Some express persistent concerns regarding fetal health, child care, lifestyle changes, or fear of childbirth pain.

18
00:02:18,012 --> 00:02:26,975
Anxiety, sleep disorders, and functional impairment are common (Romero, 2014; Vythilingum, 2008).

19
00:02:26,975 --> 00:02:42,700
The level of perceived stress is significantly higher for women whose fetus is at high risk for a malformation, for those with preterm labor or delivery, and for those with other medical complications (Alder, 2007; Ross, 2006).

20
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:52,625
For example, Hippman and associates (2009) screened for depression in 81 women who had an increased risk for a fetus with aneuploidy.

21
00:02:52,625 --> 00:03:00,862
Half of these women had a positive depression screening score, whereas only 2.4 percent of those with a normal pregnancy did so.

22
00:03:00,862 --> 00:03:06,850
Several steps can diminish psychological stress in the event of a poor obstetrical outcome.

23
00:03:06,850 --> 00:03:15,950
For example, following a stillbirth, parental contact with the newborn and provision of photographs and other infant memorabilia is encouraged .

24
00:03:15,950 --> 00:03:20,250
Addressing associated sleep disorders also seems reasonable .

25
00:03:20,250 --> 00:03:22,675
# The Puerperium.

26
00:03:22,675 --> 00:03:28,750
This is a particularly stressful time for women, and risks for mental illness are increased.

27
00:03:28,750 --> 00:03:39,800
Up to 15 percent of women develop a nonpsychotic postpartum depressive disorder within 6 months of delivery (Tam, 2007; Yonkers, 2011).

28
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:50,600
Depressive disorders are more likely in women with obstetrical complications such as severe preeclampsia or fetal-growth restriction, especially if associated with early delivery.

29
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:56,975
Prior psychiatric disorders or childhood trauma also raises the risk for postpartum depression .

30
00:03:56,975 --> 00:04:04,162
Instead, some have a psychotic illness following delivery, and half of these manifest as a bipolar disorder.

31
00:04:04,162 --> 00:04:11,550
Importantly, stressors beyond those directly related to the pregnancy also can raise perinatal depression rates.

32
00:04:11,550 --> 00:04:19,375
Tarney and colleagues (2015) identified spouse military deployment as a factor for postpartum depression.

33
00:04:19,375 --> 00:04:27,512
But, among women with a history of bipolar disorder, these elements play a lesser role in the development of mania or depression .

34
00:04:27,512 --> 00:04:35,900
Maternity Blues Also called postpartum blues, this is a time-limited period of heightened emotional reactivity experienced by

35
00:04:36,052 --> 00:04:39,789
half of women within the first week after parturition.

36
00:04:39,739 --> 00:04:48,739
Prevalence estimates for the blues range from 26 to 84 percent depending on diagnostic criteria (O’Hara, 2014).

37
00:04:48,739 --> 00:04:57,164
This emotional state generally peaks on the fourth or fifth postpartum day and normalizes by day 10 (O’Keane, 2011).

38
00:04:57,164 --> 00:05:03,627
The predominant mood during the puerperium is happiness, but affected mothers are more emotionally labile.

39
00:05:03,627 --> 00:05:11,002
They also may have insomnia, weepiness, depression, anxiety, poor concentration, and irritability.

40
00:05:11,002 --> 00:05:18,614
Mothers may be transiently tearful for several hours and then recover completely, only to be tearful again the next day.

41
00:05:18,614 --> 00:05:27,614
Supportive treatment is indicated, and affected women are reassured that the dysphoria is transient and most likely due to biochemical changes.

42
00:05:27,614 --> 00:05:33,314
They are monitored for development of depression and other severe psychiatric disturbances.

43
00:05:33,314 --> 00:05:36,852
# Perinatal Evaluation and Screening.

44
00:05:36,852 --> 00:05:50,752
Both the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018b) and the United States Preventive Services Task Force now recommend screening at least once during the perinatal period for depression and anxiety .

45
00:05:50,752 --> 00:06:01,102
Indeed, it has recently been recommended that all obstetrical care providers complete a full assessment of mood and emotional well-being during the comprehensive postpartum visit .

46
00:06:01,102 --> 00:06:09,789
Identification of psychiatric disorders in pregnancy can be challenging because changes in behavior and mood are often attributed to pregnancy.

47
00:06:09,789 --> 00:06:19,377
To differentiate these, Yonkers and associates (2011) recommend assessment of cognitive symptoms—for example, loss of concentration.

48
00:06:19,377 --> 00:06:27,189
Excessive anxiety and insomnia—even during periods of infant sleep—also can suggest postpartum depression.

49
00:06:27,189 --> 00:06:33,902
Specific factors for depression are reviewed and include a prior personal or family history of depression.

50
00:06:33,902 --> 00:06:38,314
Universal screening programs for depression continue to evolve .

51
00:06:38,314 --> 00:06:42,427
One major step forward is state-level support for screening.

52
00:06:42,427 --> 00:06:52,189
Beginning July 1, 2018, mothers who take newborns for Texas Health Steps routine examination are able to receive testing and counseling .

53
00:06:52,189 --> 00:07:04,127
At Parkland Hospital, mental illness screening is completed at the first prenatal visit using a brief riskbased query and again postpartum using a recognized screening tool for postpartum depression.

54
00:07:04,127 --> 00:07:13,152
Questions search for psychiatric disorders, related therapy, prior or current use of psychoactive medications, and current symptoms.

55
00:07:13,152 --> 00:07:23,227
Women with a history of sexual, physical, or verbal abuse; illicit substance use; and personality disorders are also at greater risk for depression .

56
00:07:23,227 --> 00:07:29,639
Smoking and nicotine dependence and obesity also raise rates of all mental disorders in pregnancy .

57
00:07:29,639 --> 00:07:38,202
Last, because eating disorders may be exacerbated by pregnancy, affected women are followed closely (p. 1149).

58
00:07:38,202 --> 00:07:46,927
Several screening instruments shown in Table 64-1 are available and have been validated for use during pregnancy and the puerperium .

59
00:07:46,927 --> 00:07:53,002
Use of one of these is encouraged because symptom- or risk-based screening alone may be insufficient.

60
00:07:53,002 --> 00:08:03,277
Cerimele and colleagues (2013) found that obstetrician-gynecologists failed to identify 60 percent of depressed women in clinical practice.

61
00:08:03,277 --> 00:08:13,614
In one study at Parkland Hospital, 17,000 women were screened during their first postpartum visit using the Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale (EPDS).

62
00:08:13,614 --> 00:08:22,002
Of these, 6 percent had scores that indicated either minor or major depressive symptoms, and 12 women had thoughts of self-harm .

63
00:08:22,002 --> 00:08:34,152
In another study, Kim and coworkers (2015) assessed suicidal ideation in more than 22,000 women screened using the EPDS both during pregnancy and postpartum.

64
00:08:34,152 --> 00:08:39,664
They found rates of depression as high as 3.4 percent during the puerperium.

65
00:08:39,664 --> 00:08:46,902
A small fraction of those with thoughts of self-harm had a credible plan, intent, and means for attempted suicide.

66
00:08:46,902 --> 00:08:49,489
Table 64-1.

67
00:08:49,489 --> 00:09:00,514
Depression Screening Tools Screening for perinatal depression alone was previously considered insufficient, however, evidence now suggests that screening itself has clinical benefit .

68
00:09:00,514 --> 00:09:09,389
One systematic review of six randomized trials that screened pregnant or postpartum women found that follow-up of these women weeks to months later

69
00:09:09,556 --> 00:09:18,893
demonstrated an absolute risk reduction in the depression rate by a range of 2.1 to 9.1 percent (O’Connor, 2016).

70
00:09:18,843 --> 00:09:25,506
However, this review included studies with and without treatment components compared with usual care.

71
00:09:25,506 --> 00:09:32,043
Another limitation is that four of the six studies did not exclude women previously known to be depressed.

72
00:09:32,043 --> 00:09:49,393
Findings from this analysis prompted an interim update by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018b) to state that screening alone can have clinical benefit, although treatment initiation or referral to a mental health care provider offers maximum benefit.

73
00:09:49,393 --> 00:09:54,056
Mechanisms to ensure adequate ensuing care can be problematic.

74
00:09:54,056 --> 00:10:07,493
Nelson and associates (2013) found that more than three fourths of the 1106 women with abnormally elevated EPDS scores did not keep their later appointment for more formal psychiatric evaluation.

75
00:10:07,493 --> 00:10:13,956
Barriers include access to care, personal perception of depression, and societal stigmata.

76
00:10:13,956 --> 00:10:23,881
Women referred to a behavioral health provider located at the same site as their obstetrical care are four times more likely to access treatment than those referred elsewhere .

77
00:10:23,881 --> 00:10:31,331
To take advantage of this, at Parkland Hospital, mental health counselors also practice at postpartum clinic sites.

78
00:10:31,331 --> 00:10:46,531
Other promising interventions for puerperal depression include home visits, telephone-based peer support, and interpersonal psychotherapy (Dennis, 2013; Lavender, 2013; Yonemoto, 2017).

79
00:10:46,531 --> 00:11:03,231
A report from Kaiser Permanente, which describes the benefits and hurdles of system-based perinatal mental health care, provides a glimpse into the possible future of universal perinatal screening and treatment (Avalos, 2016; Flanagan, 2016).

80
00:11:03,231 --> 00:11:06,143
# Treatment Considerations.

81
00:11:06,143 --> 00:11:12,893
Once a positive mental health screen result is identified, a stage-based response protocol is triggered .

82
00:11:12,893 --> 00:11:23,793
In brief, the initial response focuses on maternal and infant safety, and if possible, onsite patient assessment by either a mental health provider or mental health counselor.

83
00:11:23,793 --> 00:11:31,231
Integrating on-site assessment and access to counselors quadruples later attendance rates to mental health appointments .

84
00:11:31,231 --> 00:11:39,118
For women with suicidal or homicidal ideation, perinatal care providers first determine a working diagnosis.

85
00:11:39,118 --> 00:11:45,993
Second, emergency psychiatric consultation is sought and ambulance transport is implemented if needed.

86
00:11:45,993 --> 00:11:51,718
Concurrently, perinatal care and psychiatric teams ideally communicate closely.

87
00:11:51,718 --> 00:11:57,306
Last, the patient, her family, and clinical staff are emotionally supported.

88
00:11:57,306 --> 00:12:04,156
Appreciation for this issue’s significance has grown with the recent national emphasis on maternal mortality.

89
00:12:04,156 --> 00:12:18,856
For example, Palladino and associates (2011) found that pregnancy-associated homicide and suicide were significant contributors to maternal mortality in a multistate sample from the National Violent Death Reporting system.

90
00:12:18,856 --> 00:12:24,106
Many psychiatric disorders can be improved with counseling and psychotherapy.

91
00:12:24,106 --> 00:12:27,681
In some instances, medications are needed.

92
00:12:27,681 --> 00:12:32,606
Shared decision making is employed by patients and their health-care providers.

93
00:12:32,606 --> 00:12:38,793
In particular, women taking psychotropic medication are informed of likely side effects.

94
00:12:38,793 --> 00:12:44,306
Many of these drugs are discussed in Chapter 8, and some are discussed subsequently.

95
00:12:44,306 --> 00:12:47,043
# Pregnancy Outcomes.

96
00:12:47,043 --> 00:13:02,606
As noted, maternal psychiatric illness can be linked to untoward outcomes such as preterm birth, low birthweight, and perinatal mortality (Grigoriadis, 2013; Jarde, 2016; Steinberg, 2014).

97
00:13:02,606 --> 00:13:15,268
In a study of 16,334 deliveries, a significant association between posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD) and spontaneous preterm delivery was identified .

98
00:13:15,268 --> 00:13:23,606
Domestic abuse —another aforementioned risk factor for perinatal mood disorder—also is linked with adverse perinatal outcomes .

99
00:13:23,606 --> 00:13:26,943
Classification Of Psychiatric Disorders.

100
00:13:26,943 --> 00:13:38,593
The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, Fifth Edition (DSM-5) is the most recent version from the American Psychiatric Association (2013).

101
00:13:38,593 --> 00:13:45,343
The manual assists in classifying mental disorders and specifies criteria for each diagnosis.

102
00:13:45,343 --> 00:13:49,931
It is presumed that correct diagnosis leads to specific treatment.

103
00:13:49,931 --> 00:13:51,868
Mood Disorders.

104
00:13:51,868 --> 00:13:54,906
Historically, depressive disorders

105
00:13:55,060 --> 00:14:03,685
include major depression—a unipolar disorder—and manic-depression—a bipolar disorder with both manic and depressive episodes.

106
00:14:03,635 --> 00:14:08,297
It also includes dysthymia, which is chronic, mild depression.

107
00:14:08,297 --> 00:14:10,785
# Major Depression.

108
00:14:10,785 --> 00:14:19,497
This is the most common depressive disorder, and the 12-month prevalence of major depressive episodes among adult women is 8.2 percent .

109
00:14:19,497 --> 00:14:27,097
From 2011 to 2014, 16 percent of women had used an antidepressant in the prior month .

110
00:14:27,097 --> 00:14:35,660
The diagnosis is made by identifying symptoms listed in Table 64-2, but very few patients manifest all of these.

111
00:14:35,660 --> 00:14:38,260
Table 64-2.

112
00:14:38,260 --> 00:14:45,722
Symptoms of Depressive Illnessa Major depression is multifactorial and prompted by genetic and environmental factors.

113
00:14:45,722 --> 00:14:52,885
Families of affected individuals often also have members suffering with alcohol abuse and anxiety disorders.

114
00:14:52,885 --> 00:15:02,897
Provocative conditions leading to depression include substance abuse, certain medications, other medical disorders, and life events that prompt grief reactions.

115
00:15:02,897 --> 00:15:12,735
Although life events can trigger depression, genes influence the response to these events and render the distinction between genetic and environmental factors difficult.

116
00:15:12,735 --> 00:15:20,022
Pregnancy and Depression Pregnancy is a major life stressor that can precipitate or exacerbate depressive tendencies.

117
00:15:20,022 --> 00:15:24,572
In addition, various pregnancy-induced effects are implicated.

118
00:15:24,572 --> 00:15:30,860
Hormones certainly affect mood, as evidenced by premenstrual syndrome and menopausal depression.

119
00:15:30,860 --> 00:15:39,422
Estrogen has been linked to enhanced serotonin synthesis, decreased serotonin breakdown, and serotonin-receptor modulation .

120
00:15:39,422 --> 00:15:50,110
Concordantly, women who experience postpartum depression often have higher predelivery serum estrogen and progesterone levels and experience a greater decline postpartum .

121
00:15:50,110 --> 00:15:56,310
The prevalence of antenatal depression averaged 11 percent in one Cochrane Database review .

122
00:15:56,310 --> 00:16:06,385
In more than 1800 women enrolled for prenatal care at a single clinic, Melville and coworkers (2010) found rates of nearly 10 percent.

123
00:16:06,385 --> 00:16:11,022
A higher rate has been seen in those after antepartum complications .

124
00:16:11,022 --> 00:16:17,660
Postpartum Depression Major or minor depression develops postpartum in 10 to 20 percent of women .

125
00:16:17,660 --> 00:16:33,710
Postpartum depressive symptoms are associated with young maternal age, antenatal depression, unmarried status, smoking, newborns requiring intensive care, and stressors during pregnancy (Ko, 2017; Silverman, 2017).

126
00:16:33,710 --> 00:16:39,372
Specifically, physical or verbal abuse is a potent risk for postpartum depression .

127
00:16:39,372 --> 00:16:49,610
Last, serious adverse obstetrical events, especially those involving the neonate, are strongly linked (Nelson, 2013, 2015).

128
00:16:49,610 --> 00:16:52,335
Depression is frequently recurrent.

129
00:16:52,335 --> 00:16:58,272
Up to 70 percent of women with previous postpartum depression have a subsequent episode.

130
00:16:58,272 --> 00:17:06,660
Women with both prior puerperal depression and a current episode of “maternity blues” carry an inordinately high risk for major depression.

131
00:17:06,660 --> 00:17:18,572
Indeed, 2 to 9 months postpartum, assistance with postpartum depression was the fourth most common challenge identified in women in the Pregnancy Risk Assessment Monitoring System—PRAMS .

132
00:17:18,572 --> 00:17:23,160
Postpartum depression is generally underrecognized and undertreated.

133
00:17:23,160 --> 00:17:31,960
Major depression during pregnancy or after delivery can have devastating consequences for affected women, their children, and families.

134
00:17:31,960 --> 00:17:44,772
One of the most significant contributions to the mortality rate among new mothers is suicide, which is most frequent among women with mental illness (Koren, 2012; Palladino, 2011).

135
00:17:44,772 --> 00:17:51,747
If left untreated, up to 25 percent of women with postpartum depression will be depressed 1 year later.

136
00:17:51,747 --> 00:17:57,810
As the duration of depression increases, so too does the number of sequelae and their severity.

137
00:17:57,810 --> 00:18:10,585
Maternal depression during the first weeks and months after delivery can lead to insecure attachment and later behavioral problems in the child (Mezzacappa, 2017; Viktorin, 2017).

138
00:18:10,585 --> 00:18:16,435
Depression Treatment Therapy for mood disorders during pregnancy and postpartum has evolved.

139
00:18:16,435 --> 00:18:26,785
Babbitt (2014) and Pozzi (2014) and their associates have reviewed principles of perinatal care of women with major mental disorders.

140
00:18:26,785 --> 00:18:35,610
In general, for mild and mild-moderate depression, psychological treatment options, such as cognitive behavioral therapy, are

141
00:18:35,764 --> 00:18:37,764
considered first .

142
00:18:37,714 --> 00:18:41,826
Light- to moderate-intensity exercise may be beneficial .

143
00:18:41,826 --> 00:18:51,151
Antidepressant medications together with some form of psychotherapy are indicated for moderate to severe depression during pregnancy or the puerperium .

144
00:18:51,151 --> 00:18:57,014
Shown in Figure 64-1 is one algorithm regarding mood disorder treatment.

145
00:18:57,014 --> 00:19:01,676
Some of these medications are listed in Table 64-3.

146
00:19:01,676 --> 00:19:09,514
For women with severe depression, a selective serotonin-reuptake inhibitor (SSRI) is selected initially.

147
00:19:09,514 --> 00:19:17,901
If depressive symptoms improve during a 6-week trial, the medication is continued for a minimum of 6 months to prevent relapse .

148
00:19:17,901 --> 00:19:20,564
Figure 64-1.

149
00:19:20,564 --> 00:19:24,651
Treatment algorithm for pregnant women with mood disorders.

150
00:19:24,651 --> 00:19:27,151
Rx = prescription.

151
00:19:27,151 --> 00:19:29,801
Table 64-3.

152
00:19:29,801 --> 00:19:41,826
Drugs Used for Treatment of Major Mood Disorders in Pregnant Women If the response is suboptimal or a relapse occurs, another SSRI is substituted, or psychiatric referral is considered.

153
00:19:41,826 --> 00:19:49,051
At least 60 percent of women taking antidepressant medication before pregnancy have symptoms during pregnancy.

154
00:19:49,051 --> 00:19:58,126
In one study, approximately three fourths of women taking antidepressants before pregnancy stopped taking them before or during early pregnancy .

155
00:19:58,126 --> 00:20:06,901
For those who discontinue treatment, almost 70 percent have a relapse compared with approximately 25 percent who continue therapy.

156
00:20:06,901 --> 00:20:15,276
In one metaanalysis, women using antidepressants during pregnancy had higher rates of preterm birth and low-birthweight neonates .

157
00:20:15,276 --> 00:20:20,539
Some of the included studies failed to control for the underlying depressive illness .

158
00:20:20,539 --> 00:20:32,701
Nevertheless, in their review, Ray and Stowe (2014) concluded that the relative reproductive safety data are reassuring and that antidepressants remain a reasonable treatment option.

159
00:20:32,701 --> 00:20:42,401
Furthermore, recurrence sometime after medication is discontinued develops in 50 to 85 percent of women with an initial postpartum depressive episode.

160
00:20:42,401 --> 00:20:50,689
Surveillance includes monitoring for thoughts of suicide or infanticide, emergence of psychosis, and response to therapy.

161
00:20:50,689 --> 00:20:55,989
For some women, the course of illness is severe enough to warrant hospitalization.

162
00:20:55,989 --> 00:20:59,139
Fetal and Neonatal Effects of Therapy.

163
00:20:59,139 --> 00:21:05,826
Some known and possible fetal and neonatal effects of treatment are listed in Table 64-3.

164
00:21:05,826 --> 00:21:16,364
Some studies suggest that SSRIs pose an elevated teratogenic risk for fetal cardiac defects, and these have mainly focused on paroxetine (Paxil).

165
00:21:16,364 --> 00:21:21,126
Associations were most consistent for ventricular septal defects.

166
00:21:21,126 --> 00:21:26,489
The estimated risk is no greater than 1 case in 200 exposed newborns .

167
00:21:26,489 --> 00:21:38,176
Nevertheless, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018b) recommends that paroxetine be avoided in women who are either pregnant or planning pregnancy.

168
00:21:38,176 --> 00:21:44,801
In women exposed to paroxetine in the first trimester, fetal echocardiography is considered.

169
00:21:44,801 --> 00:21:52,839
In one analysis of SSRIs, exposure to SSRIs during pregnancy and perinatal mortality were not linked .

170
00:21:52,839 --> 00:22:03,189
Andersen and associates (2014) found that women discontinuing SSRI treatment in early pregnancy had a small increased risk of miscarriage.

171
00:22:03,189 --> 00:22:10,076
However, the risk was similar to that in women discontinuing SSRI treatment months before pregnancy.

172
00:22:10,076 --> 00:22:18,026
These investigators concluded that treatment with SSRIs during pregnancy should not be discontinued for fear of miscarriage.

173
00:22:18,026 --> 00:22:28,701
Of other potential effects, the risk of persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn is sixfold higher in neonates exposed to SSRIs after 20 weeks’ gestation .

174
00:22:28,701 --> 00:22:36,026
This translates to an overall pulmonary hypertension risk of less than 1 case in 100 exposed newborns .

175
00:22:36,026 --> 00:22:44,401
A population-based cohort study of 1.6 million pregnancies identified a twofold greater rate in exposed neonates.

176
00:22:44,401 --> 00:22:49,851
This yields an estimated attributable risk of 2 cases per 1000 births .

177
00:22:49,851 --> 00:22:59,076
In a study of more than 120,000 women prescribed antidepressants, an attributed risk of 1 case per 1000 births was found .

178
00:22:59,076 --> 00:23:08,776
The maternal risk associated with discontinuing or tapering SSRI use during pregnancy must be weighed against marginally elevated neonatal risks .

179
00:23:08,776 --> 00:23:17,301
Women who abruptly discontinue either serotonin- or norepinephrine-reuptake inhibitor therapy typically experience some form of withdrawal.

180
00:23:17,301 --> 00:23:22,201
Not surprisingly, up to 30 percent of exposed neonates also

181
00:23:22,372 --> 00:23:25,084
may exhibit withdrawal symptoms.

182
00:23:25,034 --> 00:23:29,797
Symptoms are similar to opioid withdrawal but typically are less severe.

183
00:23:29,797 --> 00:23:37,884
Neonatal SSRI withdrawal is usually self-limited, and the newborn rarely remains in the nursery more than 5 days .

184
00:23:37,884 --> 00:23:46,497
Recent magnetic resonance imaging studies show brain volume differences in SSRI-exposed fetuses compared with unexposed ones .

185
00:23:46,497 --> 00:24:03,809
Still, at this time, convincing evidence of long-term neurobehavioral effects of fetal exposure to these medications is lacking (Grzeskowiak, 2016; Man, 2017; Mezzacappa, 2017; Viktorin, 2017).

186
00:24:03,809 --> 00:24:07,822
Some psychotropic medications pass into breast milk.

187
00:24:07,822 --> 00:24:12,772
In most cases, however, levels are very low or undetectable.

188
00:24:12,772 --> 00:24:18,022
Effects may be transient irritability, sleep disturbances, and colic.

189
00:24:18,022 --> 00:24:20,147
Allopregnanolone.

190
00:24:20,147 --> 00:24:31,084
A recent Food and Drug Administration (FDA)-approved therapeutic agent for treatment of postpartum depression is brexanolone, which is an allopregnanolone analogue.

191
00:24:31,084 --> 00:24:40,409
Plasma allopregnanolone levels rise in connection with progesterone throughout pregnancy, and after childbirth these concentrations drop abruptly.

192
00:24:40,409 --> 00:24:50,897
Failure of gamma-aminobutyric acid type A (GABA-A) receptors to adapt to these changes following delivery is a suggested antecedent to postpartum depression.

193
00:24:50,897 --> 00:25:05,084
In small randomized trials, intravenous infusions of brexanolone significantly reduced Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression scores compared with placebo (Kanes, 2017; MeltzerBrody, 2018a).

194
00:25:05,084 --> 00:25:11,659
Treatment is prohibitively expensive, and further studies are needed before its use is widely accepted.

195
00:25:11,659 --> 00:25:13,172
Ketamine.

196
00:25:13,172 --> 00:25:21,384
This anesthetic drug is given intravenously for refractory depression, but studies in pregnant or postpartum women are lacking.

197
00:25:21,384 --> 00:25:27,872
Similarly, the recent FDA-approved esketamine nasal spray has not been studied in these women.

198
00:25:27,872 --> 00:25:30,447
Electroconvulsive Therapy.

199
00:25:30,447 --> 00:25:39,234
This form of depression treatment is occasionally necessary during pregnancy for women with major mood disorders unresponsive to pharmacotherapy.

200
00:25:39,234 --> 00:25:46,047
Women undergoing electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) should be fasting for at least 6 hours.

201
00:25:46,047 --> 00:25:54,034
They are given a rapid-acting antacid before the procedure, and their airway is protected to decrease the likelihood of aspiration.

202
00:25:54,034 --> 00:26:01,747
After midpregnancy, a wedge is placed under the right hip to prevent sudden maternal hypotension from aortocaval compression.

203
00:26:01,747 --> 00:26:14,159
Other important preparatory steps include assessment of the cervix, discontinuation of nonessential anticholinergic medication, uterine and fetal heart rate monitoring, and intravenous hydration.

204
00:26:14,159 --> 00:26:18,422
During the procedure, excessive hyperventilation is avoided.

205
00:26:18,422 --> 00:26:26,759
In most cases, maternal and fetal heart rate and maternal blood pressure and oxygen saturation remain normal throughout the procedure.

206
00:26:26,759 --> 00:26:32,222
With proper preparation, the risks to both mother and fetus appear to be reasonable .

207
00:26:32,222 --> 00:26:36,347
Such therapy is relatively safe in the first trimester .

208
00:26:36,347 --> 00:26:41,472
That said, adverse maternal and perinatal outcomes have followed ECT.

209
00:26:41,472 --> 00:26:54,572
Balki and associates (2006) reported a pregnancy in which fetal brain damage likely was caused by sustained maternal hypotension associated with treatment of status epilepticus stimulated by ECT.

210
00:26:54,572 --> 00:27:00,197
At least two extensive reviews have evaluated ECT outcomes in pregnancy.

211
00:27:00,197 --> 00:27:07,647
In one, Miller (1994) found 300 cases and reported complications in 10 percent.

212
00:27:07,647 --> 00:27:14,659
These included fetal arrhythmias, vaginal bleeding, abdominal pain, and self-limited contractions.

213
00:27:14,659 --> 00:27:22,772
Women not adequately prepared had higher risks for aspiration, aortocaval compression, and respiratory alkalosis.

214
00:27:22,772 --> 00:27:32,847
In another review, Anderson and Reti (2009) described 339 cases, undoubtedly with some homology with the earlier study.

215
00:27:32,847 --> 00:27:39,509
In most cases, ECT therapy was done to treat depression, and it was 78-percent effective.

216
00:27:39,509 --> 00:27:44,447
They reported a 5-percent maternal ECT-related complication rate.

217
00:27:44,447 --> 00:27:50,422
The associated perinatal complication rate was 3 percent and included two fetal deaths.

218
00:27:50,422 --> 00:28:02,372
In sum, we agree with Richards (2007) that ECT in pregnancy is not “low risk” and that it should be reserved for women whose severe depression is resistant to intensive pharmacotherapy.

219
00:28:02,372 --> 00:28:09,747
Suicide Self-harm is a primary cause of death among women during the perinatal period, and major depression is

220
00:28:09,916 --> 00:28:12,553
among the strongest predictors .

221
00:28:12,503 --> 00:28:21,616
Between 2004 and 2012, self-harm, suicide, or drug overdose was the leading cause of maternal death in Colorado .

222
00:28:21,616 --> 00:28:29,553
During a similar time period in Ontario, perinatal suicide accounted for 5.3 percent of all maternal deaths .

223
00:28:29,553 --> 00:28:34,716
Fetal loss is associated with a threeto fivefold greater risk for suicide .

224
00:28:34,716 --> 00:28:41,891
And, 54 percent of pregnancy-associated suicides reportedly involved intimate-partner conflict .

225
00:28:41,891 --> 00:28:45,266
# Bipolar and Related Disorders.

226
00:28:45,266 --> 00:28:54,653
According to the National Institute of Mental Health (2017a), the lifetime prevalence for manic-depression illness is 4.4 percent.

227
00:28:54,653 --> 00:29:01,541
The prevalence of bipolar disorder does not vary between gravidas and nonpregnant reproductive-aged women .

228
00:29:01,541 --> 00:29:08,203
It has a strong genetic component and has been linked to possible mutations on chromosomes 16 and 8 .

229
00:29:08,203 --> 00:29:17,153
The risk that monozygotic twins are both affected is 40 to 70 percent, and the risk for first-degree relatives is 5 to 10 percent .

230
00:29:17,153 --> 00:29:20,591
Periods of depression last at least 2 weeks.

231
00:29:20,591 --> 00:29:27,341
At other times, patients are manic, in which mood is abnormally raised, expansive, or irritable.

232
00:29:27,341 --> 00:29:34,928
Potential organic causes of mania include substance abuse, hyperthyroidism, and central nervous system tumors.

233
00:29:34,928 --> 00:29:38,316
These are all excluded during an acute event.

234
00:29:38,316 --> 00:29:46,916
Importantly, pregnancy frequently prompts medication discontinuation, which poses a substantially increased risk for relapse.

235
00:29:46,916 --> 00:29:53,416
Approximately one third of patients with manic-depression illness will attempt suicide during their lifetime .

236
00:29:53,416 --> 00:30:01,178
Bipolar Disorder in Pregnancy This disorder has also been associated with adverse perinatal outcomes such as preterm birth .

237
00:30:01,178 --> 00:30:07,928
Women who experience pregnancy complications are more likely to exhibit periods of mania or depression .

238
00:30:07,928 --> 00:30:14,716
Moreover, symptomatic women during pregnancy more likely have postpartum relapses that can be severe .

239
00:30:14,716 --> 00:30:20,341
Women who tend to be manic present with exacerbations earlier in the postpartum period.

240
00:30:20,341 --> 00:30:30,528
Typical therapy for bipolar disorder includes mood stabilizers such as lithium, valproic acid, and carbamazepine and antipsychotic medications .

241
00:30:30,528 --> 00:30:37,716
Treatment of bipolar disorder in pregnancy is complex and is ideally managed concurrently with a psychiatrist.

242
00:30:37,716 --> 00:30:44,166
Decisions include risks versus benefits of mood stabilizers, some of which are teratogenic .

243
00:30:44,166 --> 00:30:49,866
For example, lithium has been linked to Ebstein anomaly in exposed fetuses.

244
00:30:49,866 --> 00:31:01,503
More recent data, however, suggest a lower risk of cardiac malformations than previously indicated (Munk-Olsen, 2018; Patorno, 2017).

245
00:31:01,503 --> 00:31:07,741
Nevertheless, many recommend fetal echocardiography for lithium-exposed fetuses.

246
00:31:07,741 --> 00:31:17,866
Some limited evidence suggests that lithium in breast milk, when its elimination is impaired as in dehydration or immaturity, can adversely affect the infant .

247
00:31:17,866 --> 00:31:24,228
However, lithium use in mothers with a healthy, term fetus is considered moderately safe.

248
00:31:24,228 --> 00:31:29,591
Children born to bipolar women do not appear to suffer long-term adverse effects .

249
00:31:29,591 --> 00:31:38,966
A more detailed discussion of other mood stabilizers and antipsychotic medications side effects is found in Chapter 8 (p. 153).

250
00:31:38,966 --> 00:31:46,778
Postpartum Psychosis This severe mental disorder is usually a bipolar disorder, but it may be due to major depression .

251
00:31:46,778 --> 00:32:00,828
Its estimated incidence is 1 case in every 1000 deliveries, and it is more common in nulliparas, especially those with obstetrical complications (Bergink, 2016; Tinkelman, 2017).

252
00:32:00,828 --> 00:32:07,166
In most cases, illness manifests within 2 weeks of delivery and lasts 2 to 3 months.

253
00:32:07,166 --> 00:32:16,366
Because those with underlying psychiatric disease have a 10- to 15-fold risk for recurrence postpartum, close monitoring is recommended.

254
00:32:16,366 --> 00:32:25,978
Women with a history of bipolar disease typically exhibit symptoms within 1 to 2 days after delivery (Heron, 2007, 2008).

255
00:32:25,978 --> 00:32:34,253
Manic symptoms include feeling excited or elated, being active or energetic, feeling “chatty,” and suffering insomnia.

256
00:32:34,253 --> 00:32:40,516
Affected women may display confusion and disorientation but may also have lucid episodes.

257
00:32:40,516 --> 00:32:45,928
Postpartum psychosis has a 50-percent recurrence risk in the next pregnancy.

258
00:32:45,928 --> 00:32:57,341
As a result, Bergink and associates (2016) recommend initiating lithium therapy immediately after delivery in women with a history of postpartum psychosis.

259
00:32:57,341 --> 00:32:58,666
The

260
00:32:58,828 --> 00:33:05,553
clinical course of bipolar illness with postpartum psychosis is comparable with that for nonpregnant women.

261
00:33:05,503 --> 00:33:12,503
Patients usually require hospitalization, pharmacological treatment, and long-term psychiatric care.

262
00:33:12,503 --> 00:33:18,215
Psychotic women may have delusions leading to thoughts of self-harm or harm to their infants.

263
00:33:18,215 --> 00:33:24,853
Unlike women with nonpsychotic depression, these women commit infanticide, albeit uncommonly .

264
00:33:24,853 --> 00:33:33,078
In most instances, women with postpartum psychosis ultimately develop relapsing, chronic, psychotic manic-depression.

265
00:33:33,078 --> 00:33:35,865
# Anxiety Disorders.

266
00:33:35,865 --> 00:33:42,528
The prevalence of anxiety disorder in the prior year among women in the United States is 23 percent .

267
00:33:42,528 --> 00:33:55,203
These relatively common disorders include panic attack, panic disorder, social anxiety disorder, specific phobia, separation anxiety disorder, and generalized anxiety disorder.

268
00:33:55,203 --> 00:34:09,353
All are characterized by irrational fear, tension, and worry, which are accompanied by physiological changes such as trembling, nausea, hot or cold flashes, dizziness, dyspnea, insomnia, and frequent urination .

269
00:34:09,353 --> 00:34:19,228
They are treated with psychotherapy and medication, which may be an SSRI, tricyclic antidepressant, monoamine oxide inhibitor, or other.

270
00:34:19,228 --> 00:34:28,990
Anxiety Disorders in Pregnancy Despite the relatively high prevalence in childbearing-aged women, data regarding anxiety disorders in pregnancy are few.

271
00:34:28,990 --> 00:34:34,565
Most reports conclude that rates between pregnant and nonpregnant women do not differ.

272
00:34:34,565 --> 00:34:44,753
One analysis of 268 gravidas with generalized anxiety disorder demonstrated that both symptoms and anxiety severity decline across pregnancy .

273
00:34:44,753 --> 00:34:54,415
From their review, Thorsness and colleagues (2018) concluded that some of the anxiety disorders may have important maternal-fetal implications.

274
00:34:54,415 --> 00:35:03,540
Some researchers, but not all, have linked these disorders to preterm birth, fetal-growth restriction, and poor neurobehavioral development .

275
00:35:03,540 --> 00:35:16,128
Children with a history of in utero exposure to maternal anxiety are felt to be at greater risk for various neuropsychiatric conditions such as attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD).

276
00:35:16,128 --> 00:35:26,003
Anxiety Disorder Treatment Anxiety disorders can be effectively treated during pregnancy with psychotherapy, cognitive behavioral therapy, or medication .

277
00:35:26,003 --> 00:35:32,328
Mood and anxiety disorders coexist in more than half of women identified with either diagnosis.

278
00:35:32,328 --> 00:35:41,578
Thus, antidepressants—specifically, SSRIs —listed in Table 64-3 are often the first line of pharmacotherapy.

279
00:35:41,578 --> 00:35:49,653
As with other medication exposures in pregnancy, choosing a single SSRI agent at the lowest effective dose is preferred.

280
00:35:49,653 --> 00:35:55,315
Benzodiazepines also are commonly used to treat anxiety or panic disorders .

281
00:35:55,315 --> 00:36:01,628
Earlier case-control studies linked their use to a possible increased risk for cleft lip and palate.

282
00:36:01,628 --> 00:36:09,203
A metaanalysis of more than 1 million exposed pregnancies, however, did not identify a teratogenic risk .

283
00:36:09,203 --> 00:36:19,153
Benzodiazepines, especially when taken during the third trimester, can cause neonatal withdrawal syndrome, which persists for days to weeks after delivery.

284
00:36:19,153 --> 00:36:26,253
Importantly, benzodiazepines should be avoided in women with current or past history of substance misuse .

285
00:36:26,253 --> 00:36:29,153
Schizophrenia Spectrum Disorders.

286
00:36:29,153 --> 00:36:34,328
This major form of mental illness affects 1.1 percent of adults .

287
00:36:34,328 --> 00:36:48,965
Schizophrenia spectrum disorders are defined by abnormalities in one or more of the following domains: delusions, hallucinations, disorganized thinking, grossly disorganized or abnormal motor behavior, and negative symptoms.

288
00:36:48,965 --> 00:37:00,390
Brain-scanning techniques such as positron emission tomography (PET) and functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) show that schizophrenia is a degenerative brain disorder.

289
00:37:00,390 --> 00:37:05,815
Subtle anatomical abnormalities are present early in life and worsen with time.

290
00:37:05,815 --> 00:37:14,415
Schizophrenia has a major genetic component, and concordance was 33 percent in monozygotic twins in large database .

291
00:37:14,415 --> 00:37:20,015
If one parent has schizophrenia, the risk to offspring is 5 to 10 percent .

292
00:37:20,015 --> 00:37:28,778
Some data, including a strong association between schizophrenia and the velocardiofacial syndrome, suggest associated genes.

293
00:37:28,778 --> 00:37:38,553
Some cases are related to chromosome 22q11, but sophisticated gene mapping studies clearly show that schizophrenia is not related to a

294
00:37:38,716 --> 00:37:41,241
single gene or mutation .

295
00:37:41,191 --> 00:37:51,391
Other putative risks for subsequent schizophrenia in an exposed fetus remain unproven, for example, the association with maternal influenza A infection .

296
00:37:51,391 --> 00:37:59,866
Signs of illness begin approximately at age 20 years, and work and psychosocial functioning typically deteriorate over time.

297
00:37:59,866 --> 00:38:03,241
Women have a slightly later onset than men.

298
00:38:03,241 --> 00:38:08,066
Thus, many investigators theorize that estrogen is protective.

299
00:38:08,066 --> 00:38:13,966
With appropriate treatment, patients can experience a decline or cessation of symptoms.

300
00:38:13,966 --> 00:38:26,866
Within 5 years from the first signs of illness, 60 percent have social recovery, 50 percent are employed, 30 percent are mentally handicapped, and 10 percent require continued hospitalization .

301
00:38:26,866 --> 00:38:30,103
# Schizophrenia in Pregnancy.

302
00:38:30,103 --> 00:38:40,703
Most studies have reported no adverse maternal outcomes, although some earlier ones found higher rates of low birthweight, fetal-growth restriction, and preterm delivery.

303
00:38:40,703 --> 00:38:53,253
In a study of more than 3000 pregnancies in schizophrenic women, the rate of placental abruption was elevated threefold and that of “fetal distress”—vaguely defined—was increased 1.4-fold .

304
00:38:53,253 --> 00:39:01,553
Because schizophrenia has a high recurrence rate if medications are discontinued, continued therapy during pregnancy is advised.

305
00:39:01,553 --> 00:39:16,016
That said, “typical” antipsychotic drugs listed in Table 64-3 are associated with adverse fetal or maternal sequelae in some, but not all studies (Coughlin, 2015; Robinson, 2012).

306
00:39:16,016 --> 00:39:28,566
Because less is known about “atypical” antipsychotics, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018c) recommends against their routine use in pregnant and breastfeeding women.

307
00:39:28,566 --> 00:39:41,941
The FDA (2011) issued a safety communication concerning the potential risk for abnormal muscle movements and withdrawal symptoms in newborns of mothers treated with antipsychotic drugs during the third trimester.

308
00:39:41,941 --> 00:39:43,878
Eating Disorders.

309
00:39:43,878 --> 00:39:57,691
These include anorexia nervosa, in which the patient refuses to maintain normal body weight, and bulimia nervosa, in which binge eating is usually followed by purging or excessive fasting to maintain normal body weight .

310
00:39:57,691 --> 00:40:03,366
Eating behavior disturbances largely affect adolescent females and young adults.

311
00:40:03,366 --> 00:40:09,178
With anorexia and bulimia, the lifetime prevalence for each is 2 to 3 percent .

312
00:40:09,178 --> 00:40:18,428
Bulik and coworkers (2009) studied pregnancy outcomes in almost 36,000 Norwegian women screened for eating disorders.

313
00:40:18,428 --> 00:40:29,703
Approximately 0.1 percent had anorexia nervosa, 0.85 percent had bulimia nervosa, and 5.1 percent reported a binge-eating disorder.

314
00:40:29,703 --> 00:40:36,466
This 6-percent pregnancy prevalence is similar to the 6-month prevalence for nonpregnant individuals .

315
00:40:36,466 --> 00:40:44,541
The last subtype had a higher risk for large-for-gestational age neonates with a concomitantly increased cesarean delivery rate.

316
00:40:44,541 --> 00:40:52,366
All eating disorders begin with the desire to be slim, and women with chronic eating disorders may migrate between subtypes .

317
00:40:52,366 --> 00:40:55,566
# Eating Disorders in Pregnancy.

318
00:40:55,566 --> 00:41:03,266
Early pregnancy complication rates are increased with both eating disorders, but especially in women with bulimia nervosa .

319
00:41:03,266 --> 00:41:10,716
Generally, eating disorder symptoms improve during pregnancy, and remission rates may reach 75 percent.

320
00:41:10,716 --> 00:41:21,428
In contrast, a typical case of hyperemesis gravidarum may actually be a new or relapsing case of bulimia nervosa or of binge-purge type anorexia nervosa.

321
00:41:21,428 --> 00:41:30,478
As perhaps expected, anorexia is associated with low-birthweight, smaller head circumference, and small-for-gestational-age newborns .

322
00:41:30,478 --> 00:41:35,941
Children born to these mothers are more likely to have psychopathology at 7 years .

323
00:41:35,941 --> 00:41:43,228
Additional maternal risks associated with eating disorders include poor wound healing and difficulties with breastfeeding.

324
00:41:43,228 --> 00:41:51,103
At a minimum, closely monitoring gestational weight gain in women with a suspected history of an eating disorder seems prudent.

325
00:41:51,103 --> 00:42:00,216
Care for these women involves a multidisciplinary team that includes an obstetrician, mental health provider, and either dietician or nutritionist.

326
00:42:00,216 --> 00:42:08,566
Psychological treatment is the cornerstone for treatment in women with eating disorders and frequently includes cognitive behavioral therapy.

327
00:42:08,566 --> 00:42:14,278
Anorexia nervosa often responds to motivational interactions with meal planning .

328
00:42:14,278 --> 00:42:15,728
After

329
00:42:15,892 --> 00:42:21,129
delivery, women with eating disorders are more prone to postpartum depression.

330
00:42:21,079 --> 00:42:28,154
Women with bulimia are at particular risk for disease rebound after delivery because of body image concerns.

331
00:42:28,154 --> 00:42:30,592
Substance Use Disorders.

332
00:42:30,592 --> 00:42:46,092
According to the American Psychiatric Association (2013), substancerelated disorders encompass 10 classes of drugs that include caffeine, tobacco, cannabis, hallucinogens, opioids, anxiolytics, and stimulants.

333
00:42:46,092 --> 00:42:57,754
Their essential feature is a cluster of cognitive, behavioral, and physiological symptoms indicating that the individual continues to use the substance despite significant related problems.

334
00:42:57,754 --> 00:43:07,129
Central to these disorders is an underlying change in brain circuits that may persist beyond detoxification and characterized by repeated relapses.

335
00:43:07,129 --> 00:43:12,829
It is estimated that 10 percent of fetuses are exposed to one or more illicit drugs.

336
00:43:12,829 --> 00:43:19,929
Agents may include heroin and other opiates, cocaine, amphetamines, barbiturates, and marijuana .

337
00:43:19,929 --> 00:43:27,242
Well-documented sequelae include fetal-growth restriction, low birthweight, and drug withdrawal soon after birth.

338
00:43:27,242 --> 00:43:32,454
Adverse effects of marijuana are being delineated with expanding legalization.

339
00:43:32,454 --> 00:43:41,642
As noted by Metz and Borgelt (2018), adverse perinatal outcomes associated with marijuana use are especially seen with heavy use.

340
00:43:41,642 --> 00:43:47,904
Moreover, later childhood neurobehavioral consequences may follow prenatal exposure.

341
00:43:47,904 --> 00:43:54,442
Thus, women are currently advised to refrain from using marijuana during pregnancy and lactation.

342
00:43:54,442 --> 00:44:03,554
Women who use such drugs frequently do not seek prenatal care, which in itself is associated with risks for preterm and lowbirthweight newborns.

343
00:44:03,554 --> 00:44:06,379
# Opioid Use Disorder.

344
00:44:06,379 --> 00:44:11,204
These disorders have reached epidemic proportions in the United States.

345
00:44:11,204 --> 00:44:26,654
The rates of maternal opioid-related diagnoses at delivery (0.8 percent) and of neonatal abstinence syndrome (0.7 percent) in 2017 in the United States were significantly higher than those in 2010 .

346
00:44:26,654 --> 00:44:40,029
Because of this, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and the American Society of Addiction Medicine (2017) recommend universal screening by questionnaire at the first prenatal visit.

347
00:44:40,029 --> 00:44:47,992
Urine drug testing to detect or confirm substance use is performed only with patient consent and in compliance with state laws.

348
00:44:47,992 --> 00:45:04,042
Medication-Assisted Treatment For women who abuse opioids, methadone maintenance can be initiated within a registered treatment program to reduce opioid use complications and narcotic withdrawal, to encourage prenatal care, and to avoid drug culture risks .

349
00:45:04,042 --> 00:45:13,654
Buprenorphine also may be offered and managed by physicians with specific credentialing (Boyars, 2018; Jones, 2018).

350
00:45:13,654 --> 00:45:24,154
Available programs can be found through the treatment locator of the Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration at www.samhsa.gov.

351
00:45:24,154 --> 00:45:30,892
Methadone dosages usually are initiated at 10 to 30 mg daily and titrated as needed.

352
00:45:30,892 --> 00:45:38,379
Concomitant chronic use of naloxone with buprenorphine is discouraged given risks of precipitating neonatal withdrawal.

353
00:45:38,379 --> 00:45:45,717
Medically Supervised Withdrawal In some women, careful medication-assisted withdrawal may be an appropriate option.

354
00:45:45,717 --> 00:45:58,942
This is controversial because of the relatively high relapse rate, and it is discouraged by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and the American Society of Addiction Medicine (2017).

355
00:45:58,942 --> 00:46:10,204
From their review of more than 600 treated women, Boyars and Guille (2018) caution against this approach but concluded that assisted withdrawal is a reasonable approach.

356
00:46:10,204 --> 00:46:19,417
At Parkland Hospital, pregnant opioid users who decline maintenance therapy are offered inpatient hospitalization for controlled methadone taper.

357
00:46:19,417 --> 00:46:27,717
The goal is reduction of neonatal opioid withdrawal syndrome rates (Dashe, 2002; Stewart, 2013).

358
00:46:27,717 --> 00:46:36,292
In 2017, 69 women who were enrolled in our opioid perinatal intervention program delivered at Parkland Hospital.

359
00:46:36,292 --> 00:46:42,867
Of these women, 37 (54 percent) delivered without use of opioids at delivery.

360
00:46:42,867 --> 00:46:54,329
Their neonates’ median length of stay was 4.5 days, which was significantly shorter than the 23.5 days required for newborns whose mothers were using opioids at delivery.

361
00:46:54,329 --> 00:46:58,242
Neonatal Opioid Withdrawal Syndrome This is a drug

362
00:46:58,396 --> 00:47:03,083
withdrawal syndrome that most commonly follows maternal opioid use.

363
00:47:03,033 --> 00:47:07,333
It is discussed in Chapter 33 (p. 605).

364
00:47:07,333 --> 00:47:09,871
Posttraumatic Stress Disorder.

365
00:47:09,871 --> 00:47:14,258
This is included in trauma– and stressor–related disorders .

366
00:47:14,258 --> 00:47:22,408
The essential feature of PTSD is development of characteristic symptoms following exposure to one or more traumatic events.

367
00:47:22,408 --> 00:47:27,958
One example in obstetrics is PTSD following hyperemesis gravidarum .

368
00:47:27,958 --> 00:47:34,608
Symptoms may be anxiety or fear but can be anhedonia, dysphoria, or dissociative behavior.

369
00:47:34,608 --> 00:47:41,958
According to Yonkers and coworkers (2014), 8 percent of pregnant women have facets of this syndrome.

370
00:47:41,958 --> 00:47:45,383
Psychotherapy is one first-line therapy .

371
00:47:45,383 --> 00:47:50,483
These women are also treated with benzodiazepines or antidepressants.

372
00:47:50,483 --> 00:47:55,433
Those with concordant major depression are at high risk for preterm birth.

373
00:47:55,433 --> 00:47:57,833
Personality Disorders.

374
00:47:57,833 --> 00:48:06,708
These disorders are characterized by the chronic use of certain coping mechanisms in an inappropriate, stereotyped, and maladaptive manner.

375
00:48:06,708 --> 00:48:10,383
They are rigid and unyielding personality traits.

376
00:48:10,383 --> 00:48:26,033
The American Psychiatric Association (2013) recognizes three clusters of personality disorders: 1. Paranoid, schizoid, and schizotypal personality disorders, which are characterized by oddness or eccentricity.

377
00:48:26,033 --> 00:48:37,458
2. Histrionic, narcissistic, antisocial, and borderline disorders, which are all characterized by dramatic presentations along with self-centeredness and erratic behavior.

378
00:48:37,458 --> 00:48:46,396
3. Avoidant, dependent, compulsive, and passive-aggressive personalities, which are characterized by underlying fear and anxiety.

379
00:48:46,396 --> 00:48:53,308
In the United States, the prevalence of any personality disorder in the past year approximates 9 percent .

380
00:48:53,308 --> 00:48:58,046
Genetic and environmental factors are thought important in their genesis.

381
00:48:58,046 --> 00:49:06,908
Psychotherapy is appropriate treatment, but most affected individuals do not recognize their problem, and thus only 20 percent seek help.

382
00:49:06,908 --> 00:49:20,558
In an observational study of 202 women with borderline personality disorder, De Genna and associates (2012) noted that affected women become pregnant during the most severe trajectory of their illness.

383
00:49:20,558 --> 00:49:25,183
They are at higher risk for adolescent and unintended pregnancies.

384
00:49:25,183 --> 00:49:35,708
Notably, Conroy and coworkers (2010) found that a mother’s ability to care for her newborn was impaired only when a personality disorder was coupled with depression.

